/* ==========================================================================
   SPOKEN "WHY" SCRIPTS, PHAR 4346 DEENT (Exam 1): data
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Read aloud by the "Hear the why" button after a question is answered
   (reviews/NARRATION_PLAN.md sections 2 and 3; Louis, 2026-11-08: "do both
   tiers but prioritize tier A first"). Tier A: every question starred in
   likely_data.js. Tier B: unstarred tell-apart questions in an Exam 1 pool
   whose concept has no star. Each text is written from the question's own
   teach and option why texts and its cited slide and transcript lines; src
   lists what each claim rests on and is never spoken. Audio file of an entry:
   audio/q-<id>.mp3; a recorded clip plays only when its hash in
   audio_manifest.js matches the text below.
   ========================================================================== */
var WHY_AUDIO = (typeof WHY_AUDIO !== 'undefined' && WHY_AUDIO) || {};
/* ---- Tier A: starred questions ---- */
WHY_AUDIO["L1-060"] = {tier:'A', text:"Skin barrier dysfunction is the defining pathology of atopic dermatitis. Doctor Smith described it as skin cells that do not lie down flat, so gaps open between them, and those gaps are large enough that allergens have been found inside the skin of patients. Keratinocyte hyperproliferation, meaning skin cells dividing far too fast so the skin thickens and flakes, is the major pathology of psoriasis, not of atopic dermatitis. Excess sebum production, sebum being the oily secretion of the skin's oil glands, is the major pathology of acne.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 38 poll 1; 10/29 T 516-529, 530, 543-553"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-061"] = {tier:'A', text:"Keratinocyte hyperproliferation is the major pathology of psoriasis. Keratinocytes are the main cells of the outer skin. In psoriasis, some trauma to the skin, such as a bug bite or a scratch, sets off an out of control immune response. Activated T cells, a type of white blood cell, and the keratinocytes themselves release cytokines, the signalling proteins of the immune system, and those cytokines make the keratinocytes multiply too fast and too much. Skin barrier dysfunction is the major pathology of atopic dermatitis instead. Excess sebum production is the major pathology of acne.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 38 poll 2; 10/29 T 534-553"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-011"] = {tier:'A', text:"In developed countries, atopic dermatitis affects fifteen to thirty percent of children and two to ten percent of adults, so it is more common in children than in adults. Its prevalence has risen over recent decades in both developed and developing countries. Swapping the two ranges, with the higher figure for adults, reverses that pattern. About three percent of the United States population is the figure for psoriasis, not atopic dermatitis. A lifetime prevalence approaching ninety percent belongs to acne.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 10, 20, 31"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-032"] = {tier:'A', text:"About thirty percent of people with psoriasis have a first degree relative, meaning a parent, sibling or child, who also has psoriasis. That fits the inherited component of the disease: psoriasis has a genetic basis that probably needs environmental triggers before it develops. Fifteen to thirty percent of children in developed countries is the figure for atopic dermatitis; psoriasis affects about three percent of the United States population. Psoriasis affects males and females equally, not women far more often. It is the most common immune mediated inflammatory disease worldwide, not a rare one.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 10, 20; onset age not stated (item note, held)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-041"] = {tier:'A', text:"The lifetime prevalence of acne approaches ninety percent, and acne is the most common disease treated by dermatologists. It is set off in children by the start of androgen production, androgens being male type sex hormones, so its usual onset is at puberty and its highest incidence is in adolescents. Beginning in the mid twenties is wrong: onset is usually at puberty, and most patients still have symptoms into their mid twenties. During puberty acne is often more severe in boys, not milder. Calling it uncommon in dermatology practice reverses its status as the most common disease dermatologists treat.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 31"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-025"] = {tier:'A', text:"The F L G gene encodes profilaggrin, a protein that is broken down into filaggrins, which help build the skin barrier and whose breakdown products act as natural moisturizers. Variants of the gene give little or no profilaggrin, so the barrier fails and allergens can pass into the skin; patients with these variants have more persistent disease. Too much filaggrin and fast cell division is wrong twice: the variants lower filaggrin, and fast division is psoriasis. A shift toward T H one cells is also wrong; atopic dermatitis shifts the immune response toward T H two cells, a type of helper T cell. Raised sebum belongs to acne.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 15, 14, 16, 23, 32; 10/29 T 203-223"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-027"] = {tier:'A', text:"If about forty percent of people who lack F L G never develop atopic dermatitis, the gene cannot be the whole cause, so other genes or proteins must also contribute. Doctor Smith called F L G not a high penetrance gene, meaning that carrying the variant does not reliably produce the disease, and other variants of the gene or another protein may be involved. Saying F L G plays no role is wrong, because its variants do cause barrier dysfunction and more persistent disease. Atopic dermatitis has a genetic component, so the environment alone is not the cause, and the variants raise risk rather than protect.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 14, 15; 10/29 T 223-243"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-031"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doctor Smith called this the big difference between the two diseases. In atopic dermatitis the keratinocytes, the main cells of the outer skin, have a structural problem: with too little profilaggrin and filaggrin they do not lie flat, so the barrier has gaps. In psoriasis the keratinocytes divide far too fast and do not finish maturing, which is called hyperproliferation with incomplete differentiation. The reversed pairing swaps the two diseases. Excess sebum and Propionibacterium acnes, a skin bacterium, in the follicle are both factors in acne. Saying both diseases share the same keratinocyte defect contradicts her point that the defects differ.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 15, 19, 32; 10/29 T 294-300"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-042"] = {tier:'A', text:"Rising androgens at puberty, androgens being male type sex hormones, set off four factors in acne. Sebum production increases. Keratinization in the follicle duct is altered, so the cells lining the duct stick together and fail to move up and shed. Propionibacterium acnes, a skin bacterium, colonizes the blocked duct. Inflammation follows, with release of inflammatory mediators. All four of those choices are correct. Skin barrier dysfunction from variants of the F L G gene is the mechanism of atopic dermatitis, not of acne.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 32, 33, 15; 10/29 T 422-436, 559-573; hyperproliferation wording held (item note, OPEN_QUESTIONS), not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-043"] = {tier:'A', text:"Propionibacterium acnes, the skin bacterium of acne, makes a lipase, an enzyme that breaks down fats. The lipase splits glyceride from sebum, the skin's oil, into free fatty acids, which irritate the walls of the follicle and act as antigens, substances the immune system reacts to, so inflammation follows. Degrading profilaggrin is wrong, because profilaggrin is the barrier protein of atopic dermatitis. Making keratinocytes, the main skin cells, release interleukin twenty-three and interleukin seventeen describes the immune signalling proteins of psoriasis. Raising androgen production reverses the order: androgens act first and lead to the colonization.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 33, 15, 21, 32; 10/29 T 447-452 (glycerin spoken, glyceride on slide; slide wording used)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-029"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doctor Smith listed five drug classes for atopic dermatitis: corticosteroids; calcineurin inhibitors, which block an enzyme T cells need to become active; a P D E four inhibitor, which blocks the enzyme phosphodiesterase four; antagonists of the interleukin four receptor alpha and interleukin thirteen receptor, which block two immune signalling proteins; and a J A K inhibitor, which blocks Janus kinase, an enzyme that passes those signals inside cells. So all four of those classes are correct. Keratolytics, which loosen and shed the outer skin, are listed for acne. T N F alpha inhibitors are listed for psoriasis, not atopic dermatitis.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 17, 28, 37; 10/29 T 276-281"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-051"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doctor Smith listed four drug classes for acne: keratolytics, which loosen and shed the outer layer of skin and unblock the follicle; antibiotics, which act on Propionibacterium acnes; nuclear receptor binders, the retinoids, which change gene expression in the skin; and antiandrogens, which oppose the male type hormones that drive sebum production. All four are correct. Calcineurin inhibitors are listed for atopic dermatitis and for psoriasis, not for acne. Keratolytics, by contrast, are not listed for atopic dermatitis.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 37, 17, 28; PC 36; 10/29 T 594-606"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-070"] = {tier:'A', text:"Nuclear receptor binders all change D N A transcription, the copying of a gene so the cell can make its protein. The drug binds its receptor inside the cell. The complex either binds D N A directly at a response element, a stretch of D N A that controls a gene, raising or lowering transcription, or binds a transcription factor, a protein that controls which genes are read, and keeps it off the D N A. Blocking a cell surface receptor or a membrane enzyme is wrong, because these receptors are inside the cell. Killing Propionibacterium acnes is an antibacterial action, not a receptor mechanism.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 5, 7; PP 36; 10/29 T 639-660, 746-764"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-104"] = {tier:'A', text:"Topical corticosteroids treat atopic dermatitis and psoriasis because they suppress the activity of the immune system, and both diseases are driven by an overactive immune response in the skin. The steroid binds the glucocorticoid receptor inside the cell, and the complex changes gene transcription, lowering inflammatory cytokines, the immune system's signalling proteins, and possibly raising anti inflammatory proteins. Crosslinking D N A is the proposed action of coal tar. Killing Propionibacterium acnes belongs to benzoyl peroxide. Blocking the interleukin four receptor alpha subunit is the action of dupilumab, a lab made monoclonal antibody.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 5-7, 11, 36, 53, 64; PP 16, 19, 21; 10/29 T 873-877"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-089"] = {tier:'A', text:"Topical corticosteroids raise the risk of infection because they are immunosuppressive. The same effect that makes them useful, lowering the immune response in the skin in atopic dermatitis and psoriasis, also weakens the skin's defence against microbes, so infections appear among their adverse effects. Stripping sebum from the skin is not a mechanism given for them. Feeding Propionibacterium acnes is wrong; that bacterium feeds on the glycerides in sebum, not on steroids. Raising I G E levels, I G E being the antibody class of allergy, is wrong too, since glucocorticoids decrease inflammatory protein expression.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 11-12; PP 33; 10/29 T 680-683, 852-854, 873-879"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-105"] = {tier:'A', text:"Retinoids such as tretinoin bind the retinoic acid receptors, R A R and R X R, which pair up as an R A R and R X R heterodimer, a complex of two different receptor proteins, which moves into the nucleus and binds the retinoic acid response element, the stretch of D N A it attaches to, to change gene transcription. For acne, psoriasis and photoaging the wanted effect comes through R A R. The vitamin D receptor is the target of calcipotriene, and the glucocorticoid receptor the target of corticosteroids. Cytokine receptors, which sit on the cell surface, are blocked by antibodies such as brodalumab.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 7, 14, 17, 21, 66; 10/29 T 895-904"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-003"] = {tier:'A', text:"Optimal clinical improvement from a topical retinoid for acne comes in eight to twelve weeks. Doctor Smith said to counsel patients that they might not see optimal improvement for eight to twelve weeks. During the first four to six weeks more comedones, the blocked pores of acne, may appear before the skin improves, so the patient should keep going through that early flare. One to two weeks is too soon; those early weeks can look worse. Four to six weeks is the stretch when more comedones may appear. Six to twelve months is far longer than the course she gave.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 16; 10/30 T 20-30"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-011"] = {tier:'A', text:"Adapalene is sun stable, meaning its structure does not change when sunlight hits it, so it does not cause photosensitivity, an immune based reaction to a drug that sunlight has changed. The patient still needs sun protection because any retinoid that makes the skin peel removes outer layers of skin, and less skin left means less protection, so the risk of sunburn rises. Sunlight changing its structure on the skin is exactly the photosensitivity adapalene avoids. Being a potent contact sensitizer describes benzoyl peroxide. Raising sebum and blocking pores is not a retinoid effect; retinoids loosen keratinocytes, the main skin cells.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 16, 18, 37; 10/30 T 20-28, 117-130"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-017"] = {tier:'A', text:"Adapalene is the topical retinoid that is photochemically stable, often called sun stable. Doctor Smith said it is stable when sunlight hits it, so it does not cause photosensitivity, a skin reaction when sunlight changes a drug, and it may even be used together with benzoyl peroxide. It is also less irritating than tretinoin and is used for mild to moderate acne. Tazarotene, the acetylenic retinoid, does cause photosensitivity. Photosensitivity is listed for the topical retinoids in general, tretinoin among them; adapalene is the one marked sun stable. Calcipotriene is a vitamin D analog, not a retinoid, and is listed with mild photosensitivity.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 16, 18, 22; 10/30 T 68-78"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-037"] = {tier:'A', text:"Acitretin, an oral retinoid used for psoriasis, especially its pustular forms, has a half life, the time for half the drug to leave the body, of months, so patients must not donate blood for three years after taking it, and pregnancy must also be avoided for three years. Isotretinoin carries no such three year rule. Teratogenic risk, meaning harm to an unborn baby, is shared: both drugs are teratogenic. Dryness of the skin and mucous membranes is shared too, because acitretin's adverse effects are similar to isotretinoin's. Being given by mouth is shared as well, since both are oral retinoids.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 19-20; 10/30 T 81-116"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-039"] = {tier:'A', text:"Calcipotriene looks like vitamin D, so it binds the vitamin D receptor inside skin cells. The drug and receptor complex pairs with a second receptor, R X R alpha, binds D N A, and changes the expression of genes that help keratinocytes, the main skin cells, mature properly and that lower inflammation, so psoriatic plaques improve. Binding cyclophilin and then blocking calcineurin, an enzyme T cells need to become active, is the mechanism of cyclosporine. Inhibiting the enzyme P D E four and raising cyclic A M P, a messenger inside cells, describes crisaborole and apremilast. Being reduced to hydrogen sulfide describes sulfur.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 21, 26, 32, 38; 10/30 T 135-146"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-045"] = {tier:'A', text:"Calcipotriene acts like vitamin D, and vitamin D helps the gut absorb calcium. Doctor Smith reasoned that a drug that looks and acts like vitamin D likely raises the expression of genes for calcium transport, and may also affect parathyroid hormone, which helps the kidney take calcium back. She presented this as reasoning, since the exact genes have not been pinned down. Dissolving calcium from the stratum corneum, the dead outer layer of skin, is not a mechanism she gave. Inhibiting the enzyme calcineurin in the kidney describes cyclosporine and tacrolimus. Calcium in the vehicle is not a mechanism she gave; the effect comes from vitamin D like gene expression.",
  src:"teach; opt why; item note (her reasoning, not settled); PC 22, 26; 10/30 T 169-191"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-046"] = {tier:'A', text:"Hypercalcemia, too much calcium in the blood, and hypercalciuria, too much calcium in the urine, usually appear when the cumulative weekly dose of calcipotriene exceeds the recommended one hundred grams a week. So the weekly amount of this topical product is limited to one hundred grams to keep calcium levels from rising. Ten grams and thirty grams a week are below that limit, and five hundred grams is far above the figure given. Doctor Smith linked the calcium rise to calcipotriene acting like vitamin D, the vitamin that helps the body absorb calcium.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 22; 10/30 T 169-196"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-116"] = {tier:'A', text:"Pimecrolimus is a topical calcineurin inhibitor. Calcineurin is an enzyme that removes a phosphate from N F A T, a transcription factor, meaning a protein that controls which genes are read, and that activates N F A T so the gene for interleukin two, a growth signal for T cells, is read. With calcineurin blocked, fewer T cells multiply, mast cells, immune cells involved in allergy and itch, are less active, and fewer inflammatory cytokines are made, so the itch of atopic dermatitis eases within the first few days. Phosphodiesterase four is the target of crisaborole and apremilast, Janus kinase of ruxolitinib, and dihydrofolate reductase of methotrexate.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 23-27, 32-34, 59, 76; 10/30 T 322-328, 360-367"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-059"] = {tier:'A', text:"Cyclosporine enters the T cell, a type of white blood cell, and binds a protein called cyclophilin. The complex then binds and inactivates calcineurin, the enzyme that activates the transcription factor N F A T, a protein that controls which genes are read. So N F A T stays inactive and interleukin two, the growth signal for T cells, is not made. A complex with F K B P is the tacrolimus mechanism. Binding the interleukin two receptor, C D twenty-five, is not how cyclosporine works. Competing with calcium for calmodulin is not the mechanism either; calmodulin and calcium are what normally activate calcineurin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 26; 10/30 T 320-321, 333-353"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-060"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tacrolimus must first bind a protein inside the cell called F K B P, and that complex is what inactivates calcineurin, the enzyme that activates N F A T, a transcription factor, meaning a protein that controls which genes are read. With calcineurin off, N F A T stays inactive and interleukin two, the growth signal for T cells, is not made. Cyclophilin is the partner protein of cyclosporine, the other calcineurin inhibitor. Calmodulin is the protein that, with calcium, normally activates calcineurin. N F A T is the factor calcineurin activates, downstream of the drug, not its binding partner.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 26; 10/30 T 320-324, 333-355"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-056"] = {tier:'A', text:"Interleukin two is the signal that tells T cells, a type of white blood cell, to multiply. When a calcineurin inhibitor lowers interleukin two expression, fewer T cells replicate and respond, the immune system is less active in general, and so inflammation decreases. More T cell proliferation reverses the direction. More I G E and more mast cells is wrong, because mast cells, immune cells involved in allergy, become less active along with the T cells. Less cyclic A M P and smooth muscle relaxation is unrelated: relaxation follows a rise in cyclic A M P, a messenger inside cells, with P D E four inhibitors.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 25, 32; 10/30 T 290-303, 362-365"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-117"] = {tier:'A', text:"The black box warning for oral cyclosporine covers malignancy and serious infection: more lymphoma and other cancers, particularly of the skin, and more bacterial, viral, fungal and protozoal infections. Both follow from suppressing the immune system, which normally fights infection and finds cancer cells. Nephrotoxicity, kidney damage, and hypertension affect both oral calcineurin inhibitors but are not in the warning. Gingival hyperplasia, gum overgrowth, is specific to cyclosporine and neuropathy to tacrolimus. Hypercalcemia and hypercalciuria come from topical calcipotriene.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 22, 30; 10/30 T 396-403"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-077"] = {tier:'A', text:"Among the calcineurin inhibitors, neuropathies, meaning nerve damage, are the side effect specific to tacrolimus. Gingival hyperplasia, an overgrowth of the gums, is the side effect specific to cyclosporine. Upper respiratory tract infections and nephrotoxicity, meaning kidney damage, are listed for both oral cyclosporine and oral tacrolimus, so neither sets tacrolimus apart. Hypertension, cough and headache are also listed for both drugs.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 30; 10/30 T 408-419; item note (tacrolimus oral effects taught on slide)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-115"] = {tier:'A', text:"Crisaborole is a nonsteroidal anti inflammatory drug that inhibits phosphodiesterase four, or P D E four, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic A M P, a messenger inside the cell. With the enzyme blocked, cyclic A M P stays high, and the higher level inhibits inflammatory cells. P D E four is the main form of the enzyme in mast cells, T lymphocytes and other inflammatory cells, which is why blocking it suits an inflammatory skin disease. Calcineurin is the target of pimecrolimus, tacrolimus and cyclosporine. Janus kinase is the target of tofacitinib, ruxolitinib and upadacitinib. Dihydrofolate reductase is the target of methotrexate and trimethoprim.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 24-27, 32-33, 48, 59, 76; 10/30 T 430-450"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-086"] = {tier:'A', text:"Apremilast and crisaborole share one mechanism, inhibition of the enzyme phosphodiesterase four, but differ in route and use. Apremilast is taken by mouth and used for psoriasis, so its side effects differ, including diarrhea, nausea, headache and weight loss. Crisaborole is a topical ointment for atopic dermatitis. Saying apremilast inhibits calcineurin is wrong, because both drugs are P D E four inhibitors. Saying it is topical and used for acne is wrong on both counts. A two percent ointment for children describes crisaborole, which is approved from two years of age.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 33-34; 10/30 T 448-467"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-091"] = {tier:'A', text:"Benzoyl peroxide is thought to act in acne in two ways. It is antimicrobial against Propionibacterium acnes, the skin bacterium of acne, and it is peeling and comedolytic, meaning it loosens keratinocytes, the main skin cells, so they slough off and breaks open comedones, the blocked pores of acne. It also increases keratinocyte turnover and prevents the follicles from closing. Shrinking the sebaceous glands, the oil glands, is linked to isotretinoin. Binding the thirty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome is the tetracycline mechanism. Reduction to hydrogen sulfide describes sulfur.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 36, 19, 38, 42; 10/30 T 486-489"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-103"] = {tier:'A', text:"Salicylic acid at three to six percent is used to treat skin conditions like dandruff, acne and psoriasis. At that strength it loosens and peels the stratum corneum, the dead outer layer of skin, and opens comedones, the blocked pores of acne. Above six percent it may damage the skin, so ten to twenty percent is too strong for these uses. Concentrations up to forty percent are kept for removing warts and corns. Zero point one to zero point five percent is below the range given for these conditions.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 40; 10/30 T 541-552"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-106"] = {tier:'A', text:"Salicylism is the term for a salicylate overdose, a dangerous level of salicylate in the blood. Salicylic acid applied to the skin can be absorbed, and Doctor Smith called that a dangerous situation. A salicylate contact allergy is a separate side effect, the allergic reactions such as hives, anaphylaxis and redness. Deep peeling is desquamation, the extreme form of the peeling the drug is meant to cause. Ulceration is another separate side effect, seen with high concentrations, not a name for overdose.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 40; 10/30 T 541-557"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-109"] = {tier:'A', text:"Acne is the one of the three diseases with a bacterium in its own disease process: Propionibacterium acnes colonizes the blocked follicle and drives inflammation, so antibiotics aimed at that bacterium are part of acne therapy. In atopic dermatitis, a secondary bacterial infection can occur, but it needs its own evaluation for the organism involved; these antibiotics target P acnes. Psoriasis is treated with drugs such as retinoids, calcipotriene, calcineurin inhibitors and apremilast, not with antibiotics aimed at its cause. So the three diseases are not treated with antibiotics to a similar degree.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 41, 18-34; 10/30 T 574-582"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-111"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tetracyclines bind the thirty S subunit, the smaller half of the bacterial ribosome, which builds the bacterium's proteins, and block entry of the charged transfer R N A, the molecule that carries the next amino acid into the ribosome's acceptor site. Doctor Smith said they prevent the new amino acid from being brought into the ribosome, so protein building stops. Binding fifty S and blocking peptide bond formation describes chloramphenicol. Blocking release of the old transfer R N A is not where tetracyclines act. Binding fifty S is wrong for tetracyclines, which bind thirty S.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 42, 43; 10/30 T 600-607"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-112"] = {tier:'A', text:"Macrolides, such as erythromycin and azithromycin, bind the fifty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, the larger of its two halves, and stop the bacterium from making its proteins. Chloramphenicol binds the same fifty S subunit. Thirty S is the smaller subunit, the one the tetracyclines bind, so the subunit tells these two antibiotic classes apart. Forty S and sixty S are not subunits of the bacterial ribosome, whose two subunits are fifty S and thirty S.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 42, 47; 10/30 T 600-604; step blocked is held (item note), not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-008"] = {tier:'A', text:"Minocycline, doxycycline and sarecycline, the later tetracyclines, have better absorption from the gastrointestinal tract than the original tetracycline, which can be a reason to choose one of them. Doxycycline and minocycline are also more lipid soluble. Bactericidal action is wrong: the class is bacteriostatic, meaning it stops bacteria multiplying rather than killing them. Safe use in pregnancy is wrong, because the tetracyclines should not be used during pregnancy or in children younger than eight. Binding the fifty S subunit is wrong too, since tetracyclines bind the thirty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 43, 45; 11/03 T 34-36, 46-50"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-009"] = {tier:'A', text:"Minocycline is the tetracycline with ototoxicity, damage to hearing or balance, as its special side effect; Doctor Smith singled it out among the tetracycline facts. Doxycycline shares the better gut absorption and lipid solubility of minocycline but does not carry that ototoxicity warning. Sarecycline is set apart by a narrower antimicrobial spectrum, not by ototoxicity. Azithromycin is not a tetracycline at all: it is a macrolide, which binds the fifty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome rather than the thirty S subunit that tetracyclines bind.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 45, 47; 11/03 T 50-51"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-110"] = {tier:'A', text:"Sarecycline has a narrower antimicrobial spectrum than the other tetracyclines, meaning it acts on fewer kinds of bacteria, which may mean less disruption of the normal bacteria of the gut and the vagina. Binding the fifty S subunit is wrong, because like the whole class it binds the thirty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome. Ototoxicity, damage to hearing or balance, is the special side effect of minocycline. Being safe in children under eight is wrong, because the class should not be used in patients younger than eight years.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 43, 45; 11/03 T 46-52"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-022"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tetrahydrofolic acid, the active form of folic acid, is needed to make purines, two of the four bases of D N A. Without purines the microbe cannot copy its D N A, so it does not have what it needs to divide. Trimethoprim and the sulfonamides each block a step in the pathway that makes tetrahydrofolic acid. Blocking the bacterial ribosome is the mechanism of the tetracyclines, clindamycin and the macrolides, not of the folate drugs. Losing cell wall peptidoglycan and a membrane filling with sterols are not part of the folate pathway at all.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 49, 43-47; 11/03 T 84-89"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-038"] = {tier:'A', text:"In short contact anthralin therapy, the anthralin ointment is put only on the thick psoriasis plaques and left for two hours or less, then wiped off. The normal skin around the plaques is protected first with zinc oxide ointment or a stiff paste without medicine, because anthralin irritates and reddens normal skin and stains skin, hair and clothes dark brown or black. Using it on the whole body overnight is wrong; contact is short. Putting it on the face first is wrong, because the face is avoided. Twenty-four hours under occlusion, a covering that holds the drug against the skin, is far longer than the two hour limit.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 56; 11/03 T 169-183"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-045"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methotrexate is a folic acid analog: it looks like folic acid and inhibits the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase, so tetrahydrofolate is not made. Tetrahydrofolate is needed to build purines, two of the bases of D N A, so the rapidly multiplying keratinocytes, the main skin cells, of psoriasis lack the building blocks to copy their D N A, and their hyperproliferation slows. Competing with para aminobenzoic acid in bacterial folate synthesis is the action of sulfamethoxazole. Binding the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three describes guselkumab, tildrakizumab and risankizumab. Inhibiting J A K describes drugs such as tofacitinib and ruxolitinib.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 58-59, 48, 50, 68-69, 75; 11/03 T 212-224 (spoken slip resolved to slide: dihydrofolate reductase)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-050"] = {tier:'A', text:"The most significant adverse effect of methotrexate is cumulative liver toxicity: the damage builds with the total dose taken over time. So the total dose is watched, and once the patient reaches the cumulative limit the drug is stopped to protect the liver. Ototoxicity, damage to hearing or balance, is the special side effect of minocycline. Photosensitivity is listed for coal tar and the tetracyclines. Local injection site reactions belong to the injected biological drugs; methotrexate for psoriasis is dispensed as a tablet.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 61, 44-45, 54, 71, 73; 11/03 T 237-240, 249"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-059"] = {tier:'A', text:"A T cell, a type of white blood cell, needs a second signal to become active: C D eighty and C D eighty-six on the antigen presenting cell, the cell that shows antigen to the T cell, bind C D twenty-eight on the T cell. Abatacept binds C D eighty and C D eighty-six, so with no positive second signal there is no T cell activation and no interleukin two. Binding C D twenty-eight is wrong; the drug binds the antigen presenting cell side. The T cell receptor carries the first signal. Less interleukin two is a result of the blocked activation; the drug does not bind it.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 63, 25; 11/03 T 279-281, 316-328, 356-357"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-060"] = {tier:'A', text:"C T L A four is a receptor on the T cell, a type of white blood cell, that also binds C D eighty and C D eighty-six on the antigen presenting cell, the cell that shows antigen to the T cell, giving a negative second signal. It probably binds them more tightly than C D twenty-eight does, so a drug built to look like it binds them well and keeps C D twenty-eight off. A longer half life from an added polyethylene glycol chain describes certolizumab pegol. Abatacept does not enter T cells. C T L A four gives a negative, not a positive, second signal.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 63, 74; 11/03 T 300-303, 316-327, 547-548, 570-573"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-061"] = {tier:'A', text:"Signal one starts the stimulation of a T cell, a type of white blood cell, when the T cell receptor recognizes antigen shown by the antigen presenting cell. A positive signal two is the second, co stimulating signal, in which C D eighty and C D eighty-six on the antigen presenting cell bind C D twenty-eight on the T cell. Abatacept blocks that positive signal two, so it is called an inhibitor of co stimulation. Blocking signal one and signal three together is wrong; it works on signal two. It resembles C T L A four rather than stimulating it. Inhibiting interleukin four and thirteen describes dupilumab.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 63, 25, 64; 11/03 T 350-357"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-064"] = {tier:'A', text:"Dupilumab is a human monoclonal antibody, a lab made antibody aimed at one target. It binds the interleukin four receptor alpha subunit, a receptor part shared by the receptor complexes through which both interleukin four and interleukin thirteen signal, so it blocks both of these immune signalling proteins, or cytokines. The interleukin four cytokine itself is wrong; dupilumab binds the receptor subunit, not the cytokine. Interleukin seventeen receptor A is the target of brodalumab. The p forty subunit shared by interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three is the target of ustekinumab.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 64, 66, 67; 11/03 T 365-374"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-072"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ixekizumab and secukinumab bind the same target, the interleukin seventeen A cytokine, an immune signalling protein. What differs is how the antibody was made: ixekizumab is humanized, meaning mostly human with a small part taken from a mouse antibody, while secukinumab is fully human. Saying ixekizumab binds the receptor is wrong; both bind the cytokine, and brodalumab is the one that binds the receptor. Chimeric is wrong for ixekizumab, since chimeric antibodies carry larger mouse portions. Binding p nineteen of interleukin twenty-three describes the interleukin twenty-three inhibitors.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 66, 68-69, 72; 11/03 T 416-428"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-073"] = {tier:'A', text:"Brodalumab is a human monoclonal antibody, a lab made antibody aimed at one target, that binds the interleukin seventeen receptor A, so interleukin seventeen cannot attach to its receptor. The other interleukin seventeen inhibitors, secukinumab and ixekizumab, bind the cytokine itself instead. That receptor versus cytokine difference is what sets brodalumab apart. Calling it chimeric is wrong, because brodalumab is fully human. Binding the interleukin four receptor alpha subunit describes dupilumab. Calling it a small molecule taken by mouth is wrong; it is a monoclonal antibody, a large protein drug.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 66, 64; 11/03 T 421-428"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-075"] = {tier:'A', text:"Whether a monoclonal antibody, a lab made antibody drug, is human or chimeric changes how likely it is to cause an allergic reaction. A chimeric antibody combines portions of mouse and human antibody, and the mouse portions make an allergic type response very likely. A human or humanized antibody is much less likely to cause one. Infliximab is chimeric. Saying human antibodies cannot bind cytokines is wrong; secukinumab, a human antibody, binds a cytokine, an immune signalling protein. Chimeric antibodies are not tablets; infliximab is given by intravenous infusion. Human antibodies do not enter cells; monoclonal antibodies are too big to get into cells.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 66, 72; 11/03 T 429-436, 522-523, 570-573"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-084"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ustekinumab binds p forty, a protein subunit shared by two immune signalling proteins, interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three, so it blocks both. Guselkumab binds p nineteen, a different subunit of interleukin twenty-three that interleukin twelve does not share, so it is an interleukin twenty-three inhibitor, along with tildrakizumab and risankizumab. The reversed pairing swaps the two subunits. Binding the interleukin seventeen A cytokine is wrong; guselkumab is an interleukin twenty-three inhibitor. Binding the interleukin twenty-three receptor is wrong too; it binds the p nineteen subunit of the cytokine, which keeps the cytokine from its receptor.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 67-69; 11/03 T 469-481"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-079"] = {tier:'A', text:"Blocking interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three helps psoriasis by suppressing the formation of pro inflammatory T H one and T H seventeen cells, two kinds of helper T cell that drive the inflammation in psoriatic skin. With fewer of these cells, the number and size of plaques fall. T H two cells and I G E belong to the interleukin four and interleukin thirteen axis targeted by dupilumab. T N F alpha, the cytokine that macrophages make, is the target of the T N F inhibitors, which bind the cytokine itself. Effects on neutrophils, another white blood cell, in the follicle are proposed for dapsone and metronidazole.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 67-68, 64, 70, 50-51; 11/03 T 448-453, 477-480"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-114"] = {tier:'A', text:"Risankizumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody, a lab made antibody aimed at one target, that binds the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three, the subunit that interleukin twelve does not share; guselkumab and tildrakizumab bind the same subunit. It is used for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis. The p forty subunit shared by interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three is the target of ustekinumab. Interleukin seventeen receptor A is the target of brodalumab. The interleukin four receptor alpha subunit is the target of dupilumab, used for atopic dermatitis.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 64, 66-69; 11/03 T 480-486"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-090"] = {tier:'A', text:"The whole class of T N F alpha inhibitors carries a black box warning, the strongest label warning, for serious infections that may lead to hospitalization or death, and for lymphoma and other cancers. T N F alpha is an inflammatory cytokine, an immune signalling protein, that matters for resistance to infection and cancer, so blocking it weakens both defences. Cumulative liver toxicity and harm in pregnancy belong to methotrexate. Ototoxicity and tooth discoloration are tetracycline effects. Eye effects and nasopharyngitis, inflammation of the nose and throat, are side effects of dupilumab.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 70, 61, 44-45, 64; 11/03 T 497-500"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-115"] = {tier:'A', text:"T N F alpha, tumor necrosis factor alpha, is an inflammatory cytokine, a signalling protein of the immune system. In psoriasis, immune cells and keratinocytes, the main skin cells, release T N F alpha with interleukin twenty-three and interleukin seventeen, which drive the fast keratinocyte growth and chronic inflammation. T N F alpha inhibitors bind it and keep it from its receptors on inflammatory cells, so the number and size of plaques fall. Crosslinking D N A is the proposed action of coal tar. Raising sebum belongs to acne. Breaking down cyclic A M P is the job of the enzyme phosphodiesterase, not of T N F alpha.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 70, 53, 32; PP 21-22, 32; 11/03 T 489-497"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-093"] = {tier:'A', text:"Infliximab is the T N F alpha inhibitor that is a chimeric antibody, combining portions of mouse and human antibody, and it is given by intravenous infusion. It binds T N F alpha and keeps it from its receptors on inflammatory cells. Adalimumab is fully human and given by injection under the skin. Golimumab is also a human antibody. Etanercept is not an antibody at all but a fusion protein, a lab made protein joining a receptor part to an antibody part, and it is given under the skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 71-74; 11/03 T 511-513, 542-546"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-106"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tofacitinib, a J A K inhibitor taken by mouth, which blocks the Janus kinase enzymes that pass immune signals inside cells, carries a black box warning for severe infection, lymphoma and other cancers, the same warning as the T N F alpha inhibitors, because both suppress the immune defences. It should also be used with caution in patients at high risk of gastrointestinal perforation. Cumulative liver toxicity is the warning for methotrexate. Dose limiting conjunctivitis, keratitis and dry eye are the eye effects of dupilumab. Demyelinating syndromes, damage to the coating of nerves, were reported in cases with etanercept.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 76, 61, 64, 70-71; 11/03 T 387-389, 578-581"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-119"] = {tier:'A', text:"Crisaborole inhibits phosphodiesterase four, the enzyme that breaks down cyclic A M P, a messenger inside cells, so cyclic A M P stays high and inflammatory cells are inhibited. Pimecrolimus inhibits calcineurin, an enzyme T cells need to become active, so T cells and mast cells, immune cells involved in allergy and itch, are not activated. Tacrolimus first binds the protein F K B P, and that complex inhibits calcineurin. Dupilumab binds the interleukin four receptor alpha subunit, shared by the receptors for interleukin four and interleukin thirteen, two immune signalling proteins, so both lose their signal. Ruxolitinib inhibits Janus kinases one and two, the J A K enzymes that pass those signals inside the cell.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; PC 24-27, 32-33, 64, 76; 10/30 T 360-365, 430-450; 11/03 T 574-576"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-122"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, so tetrahydrofolate and purines are not made and keratinocytes, the fast growing skin cells of psoriasis, cannot copy their D N A. Calcipotriene binds the vitamin D receptor and changes genes for skin cell maturation and inflammation. Cyclosporine first binds cyclophilin, and that complex inhibits calcineurin, an enzyme T cells need to become active, lowering interleukin two and T cell activation. Apremilast inhibits phosphodiesterase four, the same mechanism as crisaborole. Methoxsalen, a psoralen, is a drug that ultraviolet A light changes, after which it intercalates into D N A, inserting itself between the paired bases, so cells can no longer divide.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; PC 21, 26, 34, 59, 78; 10/30 T 464-466; 11/03 T 215-220; sun audio 00:00:29-00:01:00"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-123"] = {tier:'A', text:"Abatacept binds C D eighty and C D eighty-six on antigen presenting cells, the cells that show antigen to T cells, so the second, co stimulating signal to the T cell is blocked. Secukinumab binds the interleukin seventeen A cytokine, an immune signalling protein, itself. Brodalumab binds the interleukin seventeen receptor A, the receptor rather than the cytokine. Ustekinumab binds the p forty subunit, which interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three share, so it blocks both. Tildrakizumab, A S M N, binds the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three, which interleukin twelve does not share.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; PC 63, 66-69; 11/03 T 316-323, 422, 476-477"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-001"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methoxsalen is a psoralen, a photosensitizing compound, meaning one that light changes into an active form. In the treatment called P U V A, a psoralen is combined with ultraviolet A light. When ultraviolet A light hits the psoralen, its chemical structure changes, and it intercalates into the D N A strand, meaning it inserts itself between the paired bases of the D N A. Cells with D N A changed this way can no longer divide. Blocking the vitamin D receptor describes calcipotriene. Inhibiting calcineurin in T cells describes cyclosporine and tacrolimus. Reflecting ultraviolet light off the skin is the action of the physical sunblocks, such as zinc oxide.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 3, 20; sun audio 00:00:29-00:01:20"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-007"] = {tier:'A', text:"Psoriasis is driven by keratinocytes, the main skin cells, multiplying far too fast. When ultraviolet A light activates methoxsalen, it intercalates into D N A, inserting itself between the paired bases, and once that happens cells can no longer divide. That antiproliferative effect is why psoralens such as methoxsalen treat psoriasis; they also suppress immune activity and inflammation. More filaggrin belongs to the barrier defect of atopic dermatitis. Raising cyclic A M P in mast cells, immune cells involved in allergy, is the action of the phosphodiesterase four inhibitors. Neutralizing interleukin seventeen A is the action of secukinumab and ixekizumab.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 3; PP 15, 19, 23; PC 32, 66; sun audio 00:00:30-00:01:50"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-014"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ultraviolet A, the band from three hundred twenty to four hundred nanometers, is responsible for most phototoxic reactions with drugs and chemical substances. It is only minimally filtered by the ozone layer, passes through glass, and reaches deeper into the dermis, the layer under the outer skin, than ultraviolet B. Ultraviolet B is the band tied to sunburn and to making vitamin D three, not to most drug reactions. Visible light is not the band named for these reactions. Infrared radiation comes up only as something that physical sunblocks scatter.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8, 20; sun audio 00:05:28-00:05:54"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-015"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ultraviolet B, the band from two hundred ninety to three hundred twenty nanometers, causes sunburn and produces vitamin D three in the skin, and it is the band of most concern for skin cancer. Ultraviolet A does not seem to cause sunburn, because it is less energetic than ultraviolet B; its main drug link is phototoxic reactions. Ultraviolet C and visible light are not among the bands Doctor Smith compared, so neither is credited with sunburn or vitamin D three.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8; sun audio 00:05:00-00:06:43"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-024"] = {tier:'A', text:"Both ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B can cause photoaging, the skin damage that makes skin look older, immunosuppression, and skin cancers, so those three are correct. Ultraviolet B is the band of most concern for skin cancer, and ultraviolet A contributes, but less. Sunburn is wrong for both bands: it belongs to ultraviolet B, and ultraviolet A does not seem to cause sunburn because it is less energetic. Most phototoxic drug reactions are wrong for both as well, because they are credited to ultraviolet A only.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8; sun audio 00:04:30-00:06:25"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-030"] = {tier:'A', text:"The minimal erythemal dose is the smallest single dose of ultraviolet radiation that produces clearly marginated erythema, a reddening with a sharp edge, on the skin that was exposed. It is the starting point for the sun protection factor, which compares this dose measured with and without a product. The wavelength at which ninety percent of absorption is reached is the critical wavelength, a measure of ultraviolet A protection. The amount of sunscreen absorbed into the blood plasma is a safety point. How long a sunscreen stays on in water is substantivity.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 12-14, 19, 25; sun audio 00:09:35-00:13:10"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-031"] = {tier:'A', text:"The sun protection factor, S P F, is a ratio: the minimal erythemal dose on skin with the sunscreen, divided by the minimal erythemal dose on skin without it. The minimal erythemal dose is the smallest ultraviolet dose that causes a clear reddening. So the higher the S P F, the less likely a burn and the longer the time that can be spent in the sun. Dividing the other way round inverts the ratio. The percentage of ultraviolet A absorbed is wrong, because S P F measures ultraviolet B protection and says nothing about ultraviolet A. The critical wavelength is a separate test, for ultraviolet A protection.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 12-14; sun audio 00:10:28-00:12:10"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-050"] = {tier:'A', text:"Substantivity describes how well a sunscreen sticks to the skin and stays on after the skin is exposed to water and sweat. Oils have the best substantivity but are not pleasant to use, alcohol based products have the worst, cream bases do better than alcohol bases, and polyacrylamide bases are good because they do not wash off easily. How much ultraviolet B a sunscreen blocks is what the sun protection factor measures. How far ultraviolet light gets into the skin is a difference between ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B. How much sunscreen reaches the blood plasma is a safety point.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 19, 13, 8, 25; sun audio 00:17:48-00:18:55"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-032"] = {tier:'A', text:"The S P F value indicates protection primarily against ultraviolet B. It is built on the minimal erythemal dose, which is a sunburn measure, and ultraviolet B is the band that causes sunburn, so S P F tells nothing about ultraviolet A coverage. Primarily ultraviolet A is the reverse. Ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B equally is wrong, because ultraviolet A protection is assessed separately, by the critical wavelength method. Visible and infrared light are not what S P F measures.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 13-14; sun audio 00:11:33-00:12:10"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-033"] = {tier:'A', text:"A product may be labeled broad spectrum when its critical wavelength is three hundred seventy nanometers or greater. In the critical wavelength method, a thin film of the product is scanned with light, and the critical wavelength is the wavelength at which ninety percent of the product's total absorption has been reached; a high value means the product still absorbs well into the ultraviolet A range. Three hundred twenty nanometers is the boundary between ultraviolet B and ultraviolet A. Two hundred ninety is the lower end of ultraviolet B. Four hundred is the upper end of ultraviolet A, not the label threshold.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8, 14; sun audio 00:12:10-00:13:37"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-052"] = {tier:'A', text:"A sunblock, also called a physical sunscreen, contains inorganic particles, zinc oxide or titanium dioxide, that scatter or reflect visible, ultraviolet and infrared light back off the skin. An organic sunscreen contains organic compounds that absorb ultraviolet radiation in the ultraviolet B or ultraviolet A range, or both, and convert it to heat. The reversed pairing swaps the two. Saying sunblocks shield from ultraviolet B but not ultraviolet A is wrong, because zinc oxide and titanium dioxide protect against both. Saying sunscreens contain inorganic particles is wrong, because sunscreens are organic compounds.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 20, 22; sun audio 00:18:55-00:21:45"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-044"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doxycycline is photosensitizing: oral tetracyclines cause photosensitivity in fair skinned people, doxycycline more than minocycline or sarecycline. Tazarotene, a retinoid, causes photosensitivity. Coal tar is photosensitizing. Methoxsalen is a psoralen, a photosensitizing compound used together with ultraviolet A light. All four are correct. Adapalene is wrong: it is sun stable and does not cause photosensitivity, although a patient on it still needs sun protection because peeling removes outer skin. Shale tar is wrong too: unlike coal tar, it has no photosensitizing activity.",
  src:"teach; opt why; item note; PC 18, 44-45, 54, 57; Sun 3, 8, 17-18; 10/30 T 116-130; 11/03 T 42-44; sun audio 00:00:30-00:00:45, 00:17:27-00:17:48"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-039"] = {tier:'A', text:"Patients taking a photosensitizing drug are advised to use a product with an S P F greater than forty-five, the highest threshold Doctor Smith set, because these drugs make the skin react to sunlight. S P F greater than thirty is the recommendation for skin types one and two, the fair skin types at higher risk of skin cancer. S P F greater than forty is the threshold for lupus. An S P F of fifteen to twenty-four is the recommendation for skin types three to five.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 17-18; sun audio 00:17:27-00:18:10"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-040"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doctor Smith advises starting with a high S P F because adding a higher S P F after the skin has already received one minimal erythemal dose will not help much. The minimal erythemal dose is the smallest ultraviolet dose that causes a clear reddening, so once that dose has been reached, the burn has begun, and switching to a stronger product later does little. Losing activity after one hour and skin tolerating a low S P F better at first are not reasons she gave. Low S P F products blocking more ultraviolet A is wrong, because S P F says nothing about ultraviolet A.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 12-13, 17; sun audio 00:16:32-00:17:12"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-100"] = {tier:'A', text:"Psoriasis is thought to start with some trauma to the skin, such as a bug bite or a skin infection, that triggers an abnormal immune response. T cells in the skin then join keratinocytes, the main skin cells, in releasing cytokines, immune signalling proteins, such as T N F alpha, interleukin twenty-three and interleukin seventeen, which drive the fast skin cell growth. Loss of profilaggrin letting allergens in is the barrier defect of atopic dermatitis. Androgens raising sebum is how acne starts. A reaction to ingested food belongs to atopic dermatitis, where it is not driven by I G E, the allergy antibody.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 13, 15, 20-22, 32-33; 10/29 T 301-302, 313-322"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-079"] = {tier:'A', text:"Cyclosporine and tacrolimus have many drug interactions because both are metabolized by C Y P three A four, a liver enzyme of the cytochrome P four fifty family. Many other drugs are metabolized by the same enzyme, so there are many chances for one drug to change the levels of another. C Y P two D six is a different enzyme, not the one named for these drugs. Being excreted unchanged is wrong, because they are metabolized. Binding calcium in the gut is not the reason; the interactions come from their metabolism by C Y P three A four.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 30, 34; 10/30 T 419-421"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-089"] = {tier:'A', text:"Apremilast is metabolized by C Y P three A four, a liver enzyme. Drugs that are C Y P three A four inducers increase that enzyme's activity, so apremilast is broken down faster, its levels fall, and its effect is reduced. So inducers should be avoided if the drug is to work. Saying inducers raise its toxicity is wrong, because faster metabolism lowers its levels. Topical steroids do not block phosphodiesterase four; they are nuclear receptor binders. Avoiding ethanol is the rule for acitretin, not apremilast.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 34, 20; 10/30 T 197-200, 470-472"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-017"] = {tier:'A', text:"Oral erythromycin and azithromycin, the macrolide antibiotics, cause many drug interactions because of how they are metabolized in the body. Taken by mouth they also cause nausea, vomiting and diarrhea, the worst of their side effects, and stomach pain. Binding albumin and displacing other drugs is not the reason Doctor Smith gave; she named their metabolism. Blocking dihydrofolate reductase is the action of trimethoprim and methotrexate. Being pegylated, carrying an attached polyethylene glycol chain, describes certolizumab pegol, not the macrolides.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 47, 48, 59, 74; 11/03 T 68-70"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-029"] = {tier:'A', text:"Isotretinoin is teratogenic, meaning it can cause serious harm to an unborn baby, and that is a significant risk. So patients taking it and the prescribers who give it must take part in iPledge, a system that registers every patient on isotretinoin and follows them up; women pledge to use contraception, and men are enrolled as well. Fatal hepatitis is reported with acitretin, not isotretinoin. Being a controlled substance is not the reason for the registration. Weekly blood level checks are not the reason; blood level monitoring applies to oral cyclosporine.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 19-20, 29; 10/30 T 98-101, 130-132"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-053"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methotrexate is contraindicated here because it is an abortifacient, meaning it can cause a pregnancy to be lost, and it is teratogenic, meaning it can harm an unborn baby. So it should not be used by people who are pregnant or who want to become pregnant. Being safe when taken with folic acid is not stated anywhere for this use; the drug is contraindicated in pregnancy and when trying to conceive. Third trimester use is wrong for the same reason. Low absorption is not given as making it safe; the drug is teratogenic.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 61; 11/03 T 244-247"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-102"] = {tier:'A', text:"Cytokine receptors, the receptors for the immune system's signalling proteins, have no enzyme activity of their own. Each one works with an attached enzyme, Janus kinase, or J A K, which adds phosphate groups to the receptor when a cytokine binds. That passes the signal on to the nucleus, where genes for inflammatory cytokines are transcribed. Blocking J A K cuts that signal, so fewer inflammatory cytokines are made. Calling J A K the receptor that cytokines bind is wrong; it is a kinase attached to the receptor. Making tetrahydrofolate is the role of dihydrofolate reductase. Presenting antigen to T cells is the job of antigen presenting cells.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 75, 59; 11/03 T 289-290, 562-569"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-065"] = {tier:'A', text:"Titanium dioxide makes the product a sunblock, also called a physical sunscreen. Its inorganic particles scatter and reflect visible, ultraviolet and infrared light off the skin, and with zinc oxide it is one of the two sunblocks available in the United States, protecting against both ultraviolet A and ultraviolet B. Absorbing ultraviolet light and turning it into heat is how organic sunscreens such as avobenzone work. Absorbing mainly ultraviolet B is wrong, since titanium dioxide covers both bands. Raising melanin in the skin is an effect of psoralens, drugs activated by ultraviolet A light, not a sunblock action.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 3, 20-22; drug chart Sun Protection; sun audio 00:18:55-00:21:05"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-009"] = {tier:'A', text:"Crisaborole is a boron bearing molecule, and very few drugs contain boron. Doctor Sikazwe called it a benzoxaborole, and the boron is the part that binds the metal ions in the active site of the enzyme phosphodiesterase four; when the molecule loses its boron, the drug is inactivated. Silicon is the element in the silicone emollients dimethicone and cyclomethicone. Fluorine is the halogen found in many glucocorticoid derivatives, not the atom that sets crisaborole apart. Sulfur is the element he used as an example of drug allergy, not part of crisaborole's distinction.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7, 15, 20, 24; 11/03 T 623, 683-719"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-016"] = {tier:'A', text:"Crisaborole's metabolism gives a benzyl alcohol product, and two enzymes take it to the carboxylic acid: alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes the alcohol to an aldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase oxidizes the aldehyde to the acid. The acid is then ready for phase two conjugation, the step that attaches a sulfate or glucuronide group. Every step is predictable, which is why crisaborole is called a soft drug. Cytochromes P four fifty three A four and two D six are the enzymes listed for the J A K inhibitors instead. Sulfotransferase and U G T are the later phase two enzymes. Eleven beta H S D and five beta reductase act on hydrocortisone.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7-9, 26; 11/03 T 704-711, 771-773"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-021"] = {tier:'A', text:"In the metabolic scheme for crisaborole, Doctor Sikazwe gave steps three and four as the steps that lead to glucuronidation, a phase two reaction in which the enzyme U G T attaches glucuronic acid. Step three is sulfation by sulfotransferases, step four is glucuronidation, and the arrow marked three and four gives the product carrying both a sulfate and a glucuronide, so both steps lead to glucuronide bearing products. Step one is hydrolytic deboronation, loss of the boron by reaction with water, and step two is oxidation to the carboxylic acid. Any pair that includes step two is wrong, because it is an oxidation, not a conjugation.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 9; 11/03 T 758-777 (spoken 3F4 read as steps three and four, item note)"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-024"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ruxolitinib and upadacitinib belong to different chemical classes: ruxolitinib is a pyrrolopyrimidine and upadacitinib an imidazopyrrolopyridine. They are structurally different even though both are Janus kinase inhibitors that lower inflammation and the signalling of cytokines, the immune system's messenger proteins. So one pharmacological class can hold structurally different molecules. One shared chemical class is wrong; they share only a pharmacological class. Benzoxaborole is the class of crisaborole. Neither is a steroid derivative, since neither has the four fused rings of a steroid.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7-8, 20; 11/03 T 729-736"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-032"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ultraviolet A runs from three hundred fifteen, or three hundred twenty, to four hundred nanometers. Doctor Sikazwe gave both starting figures because the literature differs, while the top of the band is four hundred nanometers in either case. P U V A, a psoralen, which is a light activated drug, plus ultraviolet A, uses this band only. Two hundred eighty to three hundred fifteen starts at the lower limit of ultraviolet B, so it is not ultraviolet A. One hundred to two hundred eighty is ultraviolet C. Two hundred to two hundred fifty falls inside the ultraviolet C band.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 12, 18; 11/03 T 815-830; visible range held, not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-035"] = {tier:'A', text:"Ultraviolet A penetrates the skin most deeply; Doctor Sikazwe said it goes in the furthest, reaching the dermis, the layer under the epidermis. Ultraviolet B stops at a shallower level, in the epidermis, the outer layer of skin. Ultraviolet C does not reach the skin, because the ozone layer blocks it. Infrared was set aside as outside the topic. So of the three ultraviolet bands, only ultraviolet A reaches below the epidermis.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 12; 11/03 T 812, 820-824, 836-840"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-062"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methoxsalen is metabolized by several cytochrome P four fifty enzymes, and it partially inhibits C Y P three A four. A drug that slows three A four, a liver enzyme that metabolizes many drugs, can raise exposure to other drugs that need it, so this partial inhibition is a source of drug interactions. Calling it a strong three A four inducer is wrong; it inhibits rather than induces. Full inhibition of C Y P two D six is not what was stated. Saying it is not handled by C Y P enzymes is wrong, because several are involved in its metabolism.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 18; 11/03 T 630-632, 961-963 (spoken 3F4 resolved to 3A4, item note)"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-064"] = {tier:'A', text:"The chemical basis of action of methoxsalen in P U V A, psoralen plus ultraviolet A light, is intercalation and crosslinking of D N A. The molecule inserts itself between D N A base pairs, and ultraviolet A light then activates its double bonds so it bonds covalently to pyrimidine bases on both strands, bridging them. The distorted D N A cannot support new cell production, so proliferation falls. Autoxidizing, reacting with oxygen on its own to form cell killing radicals, describes anthralin. Binding zinc and magnesium ions in phosphodiesterase four describes crisaborole. Hydrogen bonding to a receptor is the chemical basis of action of the glucocorticoids.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7, 17-19, 23; 11/03 T 963-977"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-067"] = {tier:'A', text:"The statement is false, because only anthralin works by autoxidation, reacting with oxygen on its own. Anthralin oxidizes spontaneously, forming an anthranyl radical and the superoxide anion, and those cytotoxic radicals slow cell growth. Eight M O P, the psoralen methoxsalen, works another way: it inserts between D N A base pairs and, with ultraviolet A light, crosslinks them. Saying eight M O P autoxidizes and anthralin does not reverses the two. Saying both autoxidize is wrong for eight M O P. Saying neither does is wrong for anthralin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; item note (poll answer read as false); DPAW 17-19; 11/03 T 938-942, 977-980"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-077"] = {tier:'A', text:"Doctor Sikazwe limited the glucocorticoid structure activity relationships, the rules linking parts of the structure to activity, to four features that raise anti inflammatory activity: a carbon one to carbon two double bond, a six alpha halogen, a nine alpha halogen, and an eleven beta hydroxyl. All four are correct. Alpha and beta give the direction in which the group points from the ring system, so the orientation is part of each rule. An eleven alpha hydroxyl is wrong, because the hydroxyl on carbon eleven must be beta. A six beta halogen is wrong, because the halogen on carbon six must be alpha.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 20; 11/03 T 1019-1075"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-083"] = {tier:'A', text:"A glucocorticoid is held in the glucocorticoid receptor by six hydrogen bonds, which are its chemical basis of drug action. They form between groups on the steroid, such as the carbon three carbonyl, the hydroxyls at carbons eleven, seventeen and twenty-one, and the carbon twenty carbonyl, and amino acids in the receptor. Four is the number of structure activity features that raise anti inflammatory activity, not the number of hydrogen bonds. Two is the number of metal ions crisaborole binds in its enzyme. Eight is more than the bonds shown.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7, 20, 23; 11/03 T 1131-1139"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-093"] = {tier:'A', text:"A higher potency topical glucocorticoid does more with each application, so it is often applied fewer times, while a low potency agent may have to be applied more often. Higher potency with more applications reverses that link. Lower potency with fewer applications is also reversed, since low potency may need more applications. Saying potency has no bearing on frequency is wrong, because Doctor Sikazwe linked the two directly. Potency itself depends on the structure, the delivery vehicle and the concentration in the formulation.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 25; 11/03 T 1168-1174"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-099"] = {tier:'A', text:"Hydrocortisone is converted to cortisone, its major metabolite, by eleven beta hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, called eleven beta H S D. It acts at carbon eleven, where hydrocortisone carries its eleven beta hydroxyl. Five beta reductase is a different enzyme, which converts hydrocortisone to five beta dihydrocortisol. Three alpha hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase converts five beta dihydrocortisol onward to urocortisol. Aldehyde dehydrogenase acts in crisaborole's metabolism, on the way to its carboxylic acid, not in hydrocortisone's.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 26; 11/03 T 708, 1190-1196; reversibility by isozyme held (item note), not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-100"] = {tier:'A', text:"In eleven beta H S D, the number eleven names carbon eleven of the steroid skeleton, and beta gives the orientation of the group on that carbon, pointing toward the viewer. Hydrocortisone carries a beta hydroxyl at carbon eleven, and this enzyme acts there, converting hydrocortisone to cortisone. Reading eleven as the eleventh enzyme of a cytochrome family is wrong; it is a carbon position in steroid numbering. An alpha hydroxyl on carbon eleven is wrong, because the hydroxyl is beta. Eleven hydrogen bonds is wrong as well; a glucocorticoid makes six hydrogen bonds in its receptor.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 20, 23, 26; 11/03 T 1190-1196; 11/04 T@722-925"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-006"] = {tier:'A', text:"Vitamin D three is made in the skin from seven dehydrocholesterol. The first reaction is driven by ultraviolet light, which breaks open the B ring of the steroid to give pre vitamin D three, which then rearranges to vitamin D three, cholecalciferol; no enzyme is named for this light driven step. These steps come from the vitamin D reading Doctor Sikazwe assigned. Twenty-five hydroxylation in the liver is a later step, from vitamin D three to twenty-five hydroxycholecalciferol. One alpha hydroxylation in the kidney is the last step, to calcitriol. Glucuronidation by U G T is not part of vitamin D synthesis.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 27, 34; Endotext reading lines 60-68; 11/04 T@2989-3430, T@4330-4410"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-011"] = {tier:'A', text:"Calcitriol, one twenty-five dihydroxycholecalciferol, is the active form of vitamin D three. The last step of its synthesis adds a hydroxyl at carbon one of twenty-five hydroxycholecalciferol, and the enzyme that does it is one alpha hydroxylase, C Y P twenty-seven B one, mainly in the kidney, according to the assigned vitamin D reading. Twenty-four hydroxylase, C Y P twenty-four A one, starts the breakdown of vitamin D instead. Dihydrofolate reductase is the target of methotrexate in the folate pathway. Carboxylesterases activate the ester prodrugs tazarotene and etretinate.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 27, 29-30, 50; Endotext reading lines 13-14, 67-82; 11/04 T@3813-3900"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-015"] = {tier:'A', text:"Pimecrolimus and tacrolimus are similar in lipophilicity, structure and mechanism, but differ most in absorption through the skin. Pimecrolimus has higher non specific binding to skin proteins, so it stays in the skin and gives few systemic adverse effects. Tacrolimus has low skin protein binding and can reach the circulation. Lipophilicity is wrong, because their log P values, a measure of how fat soluble a molecule is, are similar, six point zero and five point eight. Mechanism is wrong, since both are calcineurin inhibitors. Ring structure is wrong too; the structures are similar, differing only in small groups.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 28; 11/04 T@4645-4780, T@5890-6070"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-020"] = {tier:'A', text:"The circled group in pimecrolimus is an ester, a carbon double bonded to oxygen and joined to a second oxygen, and it sits inside the large ring of the molecule. A cyclic ester is called a lactone, so this limus drug is classified as a lactone; the limus drugs are described as macrolide or lactone immunosuppressants. A lactam is a cyclic amide instead, as in cyclosporine, a cyclic peptide. Steroid is wrong; Doctor Sikazwe stressed that a steroid has four fused rings, A, B, C and D. Pteridine is the ring system of methotrexate.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 28, 30, 32, 33, 52; 11/04 T@11280-11870"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-039"] = {tier:'A', text:"Cyclosporine is a cyclic peptide of eleven amino acids. A peptide is built of amide bonds, and an amide closed into a ring is called a lactam, so cyclosporine is classified chemically as a lactam. A lactone is a cyclic ester instead, the group found in the limus drugs such as pimecrolimus and tacrolimus. A steroid has the fused four ring nucleus, A, B, C and D, which cyclosporine lacks. A peroxide is the oxygen to oxygen bond of benzoyl peroxide.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 32, 33, 47, 52; 11/04 T@11620-11700, T@12150-12254"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-023"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tazarotene is an ester prodrug, a drug given in an inactive form that the body converts to the active one. After it is applied, carboxylesterase enzymes hydrolyze its ethyl ester, splitting it with water, to give tazarotenic acid, the active metabolite with a free carboxylic acid. Complexation with the cyclophilin receptor is how cyclosporine is activated. Oxidation of its alkyne by C Y P three A four is wrong; no oxidation step was taught for its activation. Cleavage of a peroxide bond is how benzoyl peroxide acts.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 29, 33, 47, 51; 11/04 T@6942-7125, T@30193-30300"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-025"] = {tier:'A', text:"The active retinoids share a carboxylic acid at one end of the molecule. Adapalene and trifarotene are active as given because their carboxylic acid is exposed, while tazarotene and etretinate are esters that must be hydrolyzed to reveal it. A primary alcohol is the end group of retinol, vitamin A one, the parent compound, not of the active drugs. A sulfonic acid belongs to ensulizole, a sunscreen. A tertiary amide is not the shared group.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 29, 49-51, 64; 11/04 T@7222-7330, T@30193-30300, T@43100"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-031"] = {tier:'A', text:"Methotrexate looks like folic acid and competes with it for the same cavity in the enzyme dihydrofolate reductase, which is competitive inhibition. Methotrexate has a higher affinity for the enzyme than folic acid does, meaning it binds more tightly, so it occupies the enzyme and folic acid is not reduced. A lower affinity is the reverse. A separate allosteric site, a binding site away from the active site, is wrong, because methotrexate sits in the same enzyme cavity. Being a substrate turned into a cofactor describes folic acid's role, not methotrexate's; methotrexate is the inhibitor.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 30; 11/04 T@8700-9300"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-036"] = {tier:'A', text:"In the structure activity relationship of methotrexate, the N methyl group, a methyl attached to a nitrogen, is essential for activity. It is one of the features that set methotrexate apart from folic acid. The rest of the structure is built from a pteridine ring, which should remain aromatic, para aminobenzoic acid and glutamic acid. An ester on the glutamate is wrong; the glutamic acid stays as the acid. A chlorine on the benzene ring is wrong; a chlorine is a feature of pimecrolimus. A cyclic peptide backbone describes cyclosporine.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 28, 31, 33; 11/04 T@10851-11050"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-043"] = {tier:'A', text:"Grapefruit juice blocks C Y P three A four, the enzyme that breaks down much of oral cyclosporine on its first pass through the gut and liver before it reaches the circulation. With that first pass metabolism blocked, more cyclosporine reaches the blood, so blood levels rise and bioavailability increases. Doctor Sikazwe noted that a doctor may use this deliberately. Lower blood levels is the reverse. Faster activation by cyclophilin is wrong; grapefruit juice changes first pass metabolism, not cyclophilin binding. No change is wrong, because cyclosporine is extensively metabolized by three A four.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 33; 11/04 T@13190-13690"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-047"] = {tier:'A', text:"Apremilast relies on C Y P three A four for its metabolism, so Doctor Sikazwe stressed avoiding both three A four inhibitors and three A four inducers. An inhibitor slows its breakdown and can raise exposure to the drug; an inducer speeds its breakdown and can lower exposure and effect. The same caution applies to tofacitinib. Avoiding dairy products is the rule for tetracycline, whose absorption food lowers. Avoiding folic acid relates to methotrexate, which competes with folic acid. Avoiding use with high blood calcium is a limit for the vitamin D analogs.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 27, 30, 34-35, 55; 11/04 T@13908-14385, T@15000-15120"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-056"] = {tier:'A', text:"Salicylic acid is a beta hydroxy acid. Counting from the carboxylic acid group, the next carbon is the alpha carbon and the one after it is the beta carbon. In salicylic acid the hydroxyl group sits on the beta carbon, two carbons removed from the carboxylic acid. Alpha is wrong, because salicylic acid has no hydroxyl on the carbon next to the acid group. An aromatic ring does not make an acid alpha; the position of the hydroxyl relative to the acid does. Two carboxylic acid groups describe a diacid such as azelaic acid, and salicylic acid has only one.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 43-44, 54; 11/04 T@22090-22230"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-060"] = {tier:'A', text:"Glycolic acid has minimal metabolism, and the small amount that reaches the blood is handled by two enzymes. Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes its alcohol carbon to an aldehyde, and aldehyde dehydrogenase oxidizes that aldehyde to a second acid group, giving a diacid. One enzyme, C Y P three A four, is wrong; that enzyme metabolizes cyclosporine, apremilast and tofacitinib. C Y P three A four with a glucuronosyltransferase is the pair that handles apremilast. An acyl synthetase forms the glycine conjugate of salicylic acid, not the metabolite of glycolic acid.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 33-35, 43; 11/04 T@20780-21110"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-083"] = {tier:'A', text:"Combined oral contraceptives help acne in women by suppressing sebum, the oil made by the skin's oil glands. Acne has hormonal triggers, and these products normalize the hormones so sebum oil production falls. Examples are ethinyl estradiol with norgestimate and ethinyl estradiol with drospirenone; all are steroids. Killing Propionibacterium acnes on the skin is the job of the antibiotics. Breaking up keratin in the follicle is the action of the keratolytics. Blocking the androgen receptor is the action of flutamide.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 42, 52-55; 11/04 T@30883-31600"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-088"] = {tier:'A', text:"Azole antifungals such as ketoconazole are strong inhibitors of C Y P three A four, the liver enzyme that metabolizes many drugs, and every azole antifungal shares that strong inhibition. It matters for drugs that depend on three A four, such as oral cyclosporine, apremilast and tofacitinib. Strong three A four induction is the reverse. Carboxylesterase activation is how ester prodrugs such as tazarotene become active. Chelation of dietary calcium, binding the metal ion so the drug is poorly absorbed, is a property of the tetracyclines.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 29, 33-35, 53, 55; 11/04 T@32300-32640"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-091"] = {tier:'A', text:"Tetracyclines and steroids both have four rings, but the rings are joined differently. In a tetracycline the four rings are fused side by side in one plane, a linear molecule. In a steroid the four rings, called A, B, C and D, are fused in a stacked arrangement rather than in a straight row. Saying tetracyclines have five rings is wrong; Doctor Sikazwe counted four. Saying steroid rings are linear and tetracycline rings are stacked is the reverse. Whether the rings are aromatic is not the distinction he drew; the difference is linear versus stacked fusion.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 52-53, 55; 11/04 T@35205-35450, T@36837-36940"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-094"] = {tier:'A', text:"A patient allergic to tetracycline is likely to be allergic to doxycycline and minocycline as well, because the drugs share the same core structure, and drugs that share a core structure tend to share allergy, which is called cross reactivity. No allergy because the side chains differ is wrong; the shared four ring structure carries the cross reactivity. Allergy to doxycycline but not minocycline is wrong, since Doctor Sikazwe gave the same answer for both. Allergy with dairy is wrong; dairy affects the absorption of tetracycline, not allergy.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 55; 11/04 T@36452-36800"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-098"] = {tier:'A', text:"For zinc oxide, larger particles are more effective as a sunblock. Zinc oxide is a metal oxide that physically reflects or scatters light, and as particles are made smaller, down to nano size, the product turns transparent rather than white and starts losing its reflective properties. The same size rule holds for titanium dioxide. Smaller particles being more effective is the rule for sulfur, not for zinc oxide. Size not changing its action is wrong. Nano size improving its reflection is the reverse of what happens.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 61; 11/04 T@24650-24760, T@38748-39200"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-101"] = {tier:'A', text:"Avobenzone, a dibenzoylmethane sunscreen, absorbs the full ultraviolet A range and some ultraviolet B, but the same light it absorbs also degrades it. Its photodegradation half life, the time for light to break down half of it, is about two hours, so it should be reapplied about every two hours. About every twelve hours is the figure for ensulizole. Reapplying after each swim was the drawback described for ensulizole. Once a day is far too little for a filter that loses half its activity in about two hours.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 62, 64; 11/04 T@40499-40950, T@42930-43270; Smith's different avobenzone figure not mixed (L3C-060 note)"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-108"] = {tier:'A', text:"Octocrylene, a cinnamate sunscreen, is added to sunscreens containing oxybenzone because it photostabilizes the other filters, helping them resist being broken down by light. Doctor Sikazwe called it the best performing filter for photostability, with a half life of about ninety hours, and it is oily, so it stays on after swimming. Lowering skin absorption of oxybenzone is not the reason he gave. Reflecting light like zinc oxide is wrong; octocrylene is an organic absorber, and reflection is what the metal oxides do. Hydrolyzing to salicylic acid describes the salicylate sunscreens.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 61, 64-65; 11/04 T@43958-44800"};
/* ---- Tier B: tell-apart questions without a starred concept ---- */
WHY_AUDIO["L1-039"] = {tier:'B', text:"Psoriasis treatment is based on managing its underlying pathophysiology: keratinocytes, the main skin cells, turn over far too fast, and the immune response is abnormal. Retinoids are used to affect keratinocyte turnover, the rate at which skin cells are made and shed. Modulating the abnormal immune response is the job of topical corticosteroids and biologic response modifiers. Reducing sebum production is an acne target. Replacing missing filaggrin is wrong, because filaggrin is the barrier protein of atopic dermatitis, and no drug taught for these diseases replaces it.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 27, 15, 36"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-047"] = {tier:'B', text:"Acne where a football helmet rests on the skin is acne mechanica, which is caused by anything that occludes the skin, holding sweat against it, or irritates it; Doctor Smith gave the helmet as her example. Acne cosmetica is a mild acne caused by comedogenic oils in cosmetics, oils that block pores. Acne medicamentosa is acne caused by a drug. Occupational acne comes from excessive dirt, vaporized cooking oil or some industrial chemicals at work.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PP 35; 10/29 T 474-490"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-090"] = {tier:'B', text:"Retinoids act on two nuclear receptors, R A R and R X R. Those that act mainly through R A R affect genes for cell differentiation, meaning maturation, and proliferation, meaning growth, so they treat disorders of those processes: acne, psoriasis and photoaging. Inducing apoptosis, programmed cell death, is the main effect of retinoids that target R X R, used in cancers such as Kaposi sarcoma. Blocking I G E production is wrong; I G E is regulated by signalling proteins from helper T cells. Binding the glucocorticoid response element is wrong; retinoid receptors bind the retinoic acid response element, the stretch of D N A they attach to.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 14, 7; PP 16; 10/29 T 880-904 (spoken RXR slip resolved to RAR, item note)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L1-091"] = {tier:'B', text:"Retinoids act through two families of nuclear receptors, R A R and R X R. Retinoids that target R X R predominantly induce apoptosis, programmed cell death, so they are used mostly to treat cancers such as Kaposi sarcoma, a skin cancer. Retinoids that target R A R instead mainly affect differentiation and proliferation, which is the effect wanted in skin disease. Promoting collagen synthesis is a retinoid effect in aging skin, not the R X R cancer effect. Suppressing sebum production is not the R X R effect. Increasing keratinocyte cohesion is the reverse; retinoids decrease the cohesion between skin cells.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 14-15; 10/29 T 892-901"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-018"] = {tier:'B', text:"Adapalene is the topical retinoid that may be used together with benzoyl peroxide. It is photochemically stable, so it is not broken down by light, and it is less irritating than tretinoin. Doctor Smith said the other retinoids should not be used together with benzoyl peroxide, because the combination is too irritating to the skin, which rules out tretinoin and tazarotene here. Isotretinoin is wrong for another reason: it is an oral retinoid for severe cystic acne, not a topical product to combine.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18-19; 10/30 T 71-73"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-019"] = {tier:'B', text:"Adapalene is a derivative of naphthoic acid that resembles retinoic acid and binds the same receptors. Compared with tretinoin it is less irritating, and it is used for patients with mild to moderate acne. More irritating and for severe cystic acne is wrong; severe cystic acne is the use of oral isotretinoin. More teratogenic and given orally is wrong, because adapalene is topical; the oral retinoids are isotretinoin and acitretin. Photosensitizing and used for plaque psoriasis describes tazarotene, not adapalene.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18-20; 10/30 T 68-75"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-020"] = {tier:'B', text:"Topical tazarotene is used for psoriasis, for mild to moderately severe facial acne, and for wrinkles, so all three of those are correct. Among the topical retinoids, it is the one that covers psoriasis as well as acne and wrinkles. Atopic dermatitis is wrong; tazarotene is not used for it, and the atopic dermatitis drugs covered alongside it were the topical calcineurin inhibitors and crisaborole.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18, 27, 33; 10/30 T 75-81"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-021"] = {tier:'B', text:"What separates tazarotene from adapalene is that tazarotene causes photosensitivity, a reaction of the skin to sunlight after the drug is applied, while adapalene is photochemically stable and does not. Tazarotene is also used for psoriasis and wrinkles, while adapalene is for mild to moderate acne and can be combined with benzoyl peroxide. Saying tazarotene is given orally is wrong; both are topical retinoids. Saying tazarotene is less irritating is wrong; less irritating than tretinoin is said of adapalene. Binding the vitamin D receptor describes calcipotriene, not a retinoid.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18, 21; 10/30 T 68-81"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-023"] = {tier:'B', text:"The best candidate for oral isotretinoin has severe cystic acne that has resisted standard therapies. Isotretinoin is an oral retinoid kept for significant disease; it appears to shrink the sebaceous glands, the oil glands, and reduce their function. Mild comedonal acne fits a topical retinoid such as adapalene instead. Pustular psoriasis is the use of oral acitretin. Mild atopic dermatitis in a child is wrong twice: isotretinoin is for acne, and it is avoided in children.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18-20; 10/30 T 81-84, 96-98"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-031"] = {tier:'B', text:"Oral acitretin is used for psoriasis, especially its pustular forms. It is a metabolite of the aromatic retinoid etretinate, and it is the second oral retinoid alongside isotretinoin. Severe cystic acne is the indication for isotretinoin, not acitretin. Mild to moderate atopic dermatitis in adults is wrong; the atopic dermatitis drugs taught with these were the topical calcineurin inhibitors and crisaborole. Wrinkles and photoaging are a use of topical tazarotene.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 18-20, 27, 33; 10/30 T 101-104"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-061"] = {tier:'B', text:"Each oral calcineurin inhibitor first binds its own partner protein inside the cell: cyclosporine binds cyclophilin, and tacrolimus binds F K B P. Each complex then binds and inactivates calcineurin, the enzyme that activates N F A T, a transcription factor, meaning a protein that controls which genes are read. N F A T stays inactive, so interleukin two, a signal T cells need to multiply, is not made. Swapping the partners is wrong. Both binding cyclophilin is wrong, because tacrolimus binds a different protein. Both binding N F A T directly is wrong; the complexes bind calcineurin, not N F A T.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 26; 10/30 T 333-355"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-063"] = {tier:'B', text:"Topical pimecrolimus and topical tacrolimus are approved for atopic dermatitis, in adults and in children older than two. They inhibit the activation of T cells and mast cells, two kinds of immune cell, in the skin, blocking the production of inflammatory cytokines, the immune signalling proteins, and both relieve itching after the first few days of treatment. Severe cystic acne is the use of oral isotretinoin. Pustular psoriasis is the use of oral acitretin. Warts and corns are treated with high strength salicylic acid, up to forty percent.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 27, 19-20, 40; 10/30 T 357-377; psoriasis label status held (item note), not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-065"] = {tier:'B', text:"Pimecrolimus is better at staying in the skin than tacrolimus, so less of it is absorbed into the circulation. That is the advantage, because the aim is to lower T cell activity in the skin without suppressing the immune system throughout the body. Approval for infants under two is wrong; neither drug is approved under two years of age. No burning on application is wrong, because burning is the most common adverse effect of the topical calcineurin inhibitors. Inhibiting the enzyme P D E four as well is wrong; both are calcineurin inhibitors, and P D E four is the target of crisaborole.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 27-28, 33; 10/30 T 367-377; item note (formulation wording not used)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-070"] = {tier:'B', text:"Oral cyclosporine is used for severe psoriasis; Doctor Smith described using it by mouth for bad psoriasis. The topical calcineurin inhibitors, pimecrolimus and tacrolimus, are the ones used on the skin for atopic dermatitis. Mild acne is not a cyclosporine use. Warts and corns are treated with high strength salicylic acid. Severe cystic acne is the use of oral isotretinoin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 27, 29-30, 19, 40; 10/30 T 384-386, 396-398"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-076"] = {tier:'B', text:"Gingival hyperplasia, an overgrowth of the gums, is the side effect specific to cyclosporine among the calcineurin inhibitors. Neuropathies, meaning nerve damage, are the side effect specific to tacrolimus. Nephrotoxicity, meaning kidney damage, and upper respiratory tract infections are listed for both cyclosporine and tacrolimus, so neither sets cyclosporine apart. Both drugs also carry the same black box warning, the strongest label warning, for lymphoma and other cancers and for serious infections.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 30; 10/30 T 408-419"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-083"] = {tier:'B', text:"Crisaborole two percent ointment, a topical inhibitor of the enzyme phosphodiesterase four, or P D E four, is approved for mild to moderate atopic dermatitis in patients two years of age and older. Any age with severe chronic plaque psoriasis is wrong; the P D E four inhibitor for psoriasis is apremilast, which is taken by mouth. Age twelve or older with cystic acne is wrong; severe cystic acne is treated with oral isotretinoin. Under two with mild atopic dermatitis is wrong, because the approval starts at two years of age.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 33-34, 19; 10/30 T 448-451"};
WHY_AUDIO["L2-114"] = {tier:'B', text:"At the bacterial ribosome, tetracyclines bind the thirty S subunit, the smaller part, and block the charged transfer R N A, which carries the next amino acid, from entering. Macrolides, such as erythromycin and azithromycin, bind the fifty S subunit, the larger part, as chloramphenicol does. Swapping the subunits reverses the two classes. Both binding thirty S at different steps is wrong, because macrolides bind fifty S. Both acting on the cell wall is wrong; both act on the ribosome, stopping the bacterium from making proteins.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 42, 47; 10/30 T 600-607; macrolide step held, not spoken"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-016"] = {tier:'B', text:"Minocycline is a tetracycline, and the tetracyclines bind the thirty S subunit of the bacterial ribosome, blocking the transfer R N A from binding the acceptor site so the protein cannot be built. Erythromycin and azithromycin are macrolides, which attach to the fifty S subunit instead. Clindamycin also attaches to the fifty S subunit. So among the acne antibiotics that act on the ribosome, only the tetracyclines, minocycline, doxycycline and sarecycline, bind thirty S.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 43, 46-47; 11/03 T 29-31, 53-55, 66-67"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-042"] = {tier:'B', text:"Shale tar, also called ichthammol, is a petroleum product like coal tar but a different fraction of its compounds. It might be preferred because it has no photosensitizing activity, unlike coal tar, and it is also less irritating. It gives symptomatic relief and helps itching. Saying clinical studies prove it works is wrong; no clinical studies have shown its effectiveness. Saying its mechanism is well defined is wrong; its mechanism is unknown. Saying it has no odor is wrong too; neither coal tar nor shale products smell good.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 57; 11/03 T 184-191, 210-211"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-047"] = {tier:'B', text:"Trimethoprim and methotrexate both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, but in different organisms. Trimethoprim inhibits the bacterial enzyme, so the microbe cannot make tetrahydrofolic acid and cannot divide. Methotrexate is not used against a microbe; it inhibits the enzyme in the patient's own cells, so psoriatic skin cells cannot proliferate. Trimethoprim is not a monoclonal antibody, a lab made antibody drug; it is one part of an oral antibiotic product with sulfamethoxazole. Blocking the para aminobenzoic acid step is the action of sulfamethoxazole. Binding the thirty S subunit is the tetracycline mechanism.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 48, 59, 43; 11/03 T 9-11, 77-89, 213-223"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-076"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ixekizumab binds the interleukin seventeen A cytokine itself, and guselkumab binds the p nineteen subunit of the interleukin twenty-three cytokine, so both bind a cytokine and both are correct. A cytokine, an immune signalling protein, that is bound this way cannot reach its receptor. Brodalumab is wrong, because it binds the interleukin seventeen receptor A instead. Dupilumab is wrong as well, because it binds the interleukin four receptor alpha subunit, part of the receptors for interleukin four and interleukin thirteen.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 64, 66-68; 11/03 T 369-372, 420-431, 472-475"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-077"] = {tier:'B', text:"Secukinumab, brodalumab and guselkumab are human monoclonal antibodies, made entirely of human antibody sequence, so all three are correct. Secukinumab binds the interleukin seventeen A cytokine, brodalumab binds the interleukin seventeen receptor A, and guselkumab binds the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three. Tildrakizumab, A S M N, is wrong: it is humanized, meaning mostly human with a small part taken from a mouse antibody, not fully human. Ixekizumab and risankizumab are humanized as well.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 66, 68-69; 11/03 T 410-422, 470-481"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-085"] = {tier:'B', text:"Guselkumab and tildrakizumab, A S M N, both bind the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three, so what separates them is how the antibody was made. Guselkumab is a fully human lab made antibody, and tildrakizumab is humanized, mostly human with a small part taken from a mouse antibody. Each of these antibodies is told apart by whether it is human or humanized, and by what it binds. The reversed pairing swaps the two. Tildrakizumab binding p forty is wrong; both bind p nineteen. Calling tildrakizumab a fusion protein is wrong; it is a monoclonal antibody.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 68; 11/03 T 469-477"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-086"] = {tier:'B', text:"Risankizumab is the interleukin twenty-three inhibitor given either by injection under the skin or intravenously, and it is used for moderate to severe plaque psoriasis, a slightly different indication for more significant disease. It is a humanized antibody that binds the p nineteen subunit of interleukin twenty-three. Guselkumab and tildrakizumab, A S M N, bind the same subunit, but no intravenous route or separate indication was given for them. Ustekinumab is wrong: it inhibits both interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three through their shared p forty subunit, and it is given under the skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 67-69; 11/03 T 480-483"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-098"] = {tier:'B', text:"Adalimumab does not share the infusion reaction profile of infliximab because it is a fully human antibody to T N F alpha, so it is less antigenic, meaning less likely to provoke the immune system into making antibodies against it. Infliximab is chimeric, part mouse and part human, which is behind its infusion reactions. Its most common side effects are local injection site reactions, along with an increased risk of infection. Saying it does not bind T N F alpha is wrong; it is an antibody to T N F. A fusion protein describes etanercept. Intravenous infusion is wrong; adalimumab is given under the skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 71-73; 11/03 T 542-545"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-104"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ruxolitinib is the J A K inhibitor that acts on two subtypes of Janus kinase, J A K one and J A K two. Janus kinases are enzymes that pass immune signals inside the cell, and these small molecule drugs enter the cell to reach them. Tofacitinib is also a J A K inhibitor, but no subtype pair was given for it. Abatacept is not a J A K inhibitor; it binds C D eighty and C D eighty-six on antigen presenting cells. Ustekinumab is not one either; it is an antibody that binds the p forty subunit of interleukin twelve and interleukin twenty-three.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 63, 67, 76; 11/03 T 569-577"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-105"] = {tier:'B', text:"Upadacitinib binds more Janus kinase targets than tofacitinib or ruxolitinib: J A K one and J A K two, and possibly J A K three and T Y K two. But Doctor Smith said it is still not known whether it is more effective, and its selectivity is still unsettled. Binding T N F alpha outside the cell is wrong; it is a small molecule J A K inhibitor that acts inside the cell. Being proven more effective is wrong, because that is still not known. Being a humanized antibody is wrong, because the J A K inhibitors are small molecules, not antibodies.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 76; 11/03 T 569-578"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-107"] = {tier:'B', text:"Adalimumab and etanercept are T N F alpha inhibitors, and the whole T N F alpha inhibitor class carries a black box warning, the strongest label warning, for serious infection and for lymphoma and other cancers. Tofacitinib, a J A K inhibitor, carries the same warning. So those three are correct. Secukinumab is wrong: its stated risks are infection, so patients are first checked for tuberculosis, inflammation of the nose and throat, and flares of Crohn disease, but no black box warning was given for it.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 66, 70, 76; 11/03 T 413-416, 497-500, 579-586"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-111"] = {tier:'B', text:"Minocycline and sarecycline may be less photosensitizing than tetracycline or doxycycline. Photosensitivity in fair skinned people, burning quickly and badly in the sun, is a side effect of the oral tetracyclines as a class, and these two may cause less of it. Pairs that include doxycycline are wrong, because doxycycline is one of the two drugs the comparison is made against. The pair with tetracycline is wrong for the same reason, since tetracycline is the other.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 44-45; 11/03 T 42-44, 51-52"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-113"] = {tier:'B', text:"Doxycycline and minocycline are more lipid soluble, meaning more fat soluble, so they penetrate the pilosebaceous follicle more efficiently. The pilosebaceous unit, the hair follicle with its oil gland, is where acne develops, so a drug that enters it well reaches the disease. Topical application is wrong; the tetracyclines here are given by mouth. Binding the fifty S subunit is wrong; tetracyclines bind thirty S, and that does not explain follicle entry anyway. Killing bacteria is wrong, because the tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, keeping bacteria from multiplying.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 43-45; PP 30; 10/29 T 408-410; 11/03 T 51-52"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-118"] = {tier:'B', text:"Oral cyclosporine carries a black box warning, the strongest label warning, for lymphoma and other cancers and for serious infections, because it suppresses the immune system. Among the drugs covered, the same kind of warning is carried by the T N F alpha inhibitor class and by tofacitinib. Injected dupilumab is wrong; its adverse effects are generally mild, such as inflammation of the nose and throat, headache and eye effects. Topical crisaborole is wrong; its notable risk is a hypersensitivity reaction where it is applied, in less than one percent. Secukinumab is wrong; its risks are infection and nasopharyngitis, with no boxed warning given.",
  src:"teach; opt why; PC 30, 33, 64, 66, 70, 76; 10/30 T 396-398; 11/03 T 497-500, 579-586"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-124"] = {tier:'B', text:"Etanercept is a fusion protein: two p seventy-five T N F receptors linked to the F C fragment, the constant fragment, of a human I G G one antibody. Infliximab is a chimeric antibody, combining mouse and human I G G one portions. Adalimumab is a fully human I G G one antibody. Certolizumab pegol is the F A B fragment, the antigen binding fragment, of a humanized antibody, linked to polyethylene glycol. Whatever the structure, each binds T N F alpha so it cannot reach its receptors on inflammatory cells.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; PC 70-74; 11/03 T 493-505"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3-125"] = {tier:'B', text:"Tretinoin is all trans retinoic acid, and it also comes as a microsphere formulation that releases the drug over time. Adapalene is sun stable and can be used with benzoyl peroxide. Tazarotene is used for psoriasis, acne and wrinkles, and it is photosensitizing. Isotretinoin is taken by mouth for severe cystic acne that has resisted other treatment. Acitretin is taken by mouth for psoriasis, especially the pustular forms. All five act on the retinoic acid receptors in the nucleus to change gene transcription.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; PC 14, 17-20"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-028"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ruxolitinib has active hydroxylated metabolites, meaning some of the products made when the body adds a hydroxyl group to it still act as drugs, while upadacitinib has no active metabolites. That is the difference Doctor Sikazwe drew; otherwise both act locally because of their formulation, they have different half lives, and both are C Y P three A four substrates. The reverse pairing is wrong, because upadacitinib has no active metabolites. Active glucuronide metabolites are wrong; glucuronides belong to crisaborole's scheme. Neither forming metabolites is wrong, because both are metabolized by three A four.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 8-9; 11/03 T 749-757"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-050"] = {tier:'B', text:"Coal tar is the photosensitizing tar: it sensitizes the skin to light, which is why it may be used together with ultraviolet B phototherapy. Shale tar, also called ichthammol, does not photosensitize. Pine tar and birch tar are wood tars, and they do not photosensitize either. Not photosensitizing is the advantage of the shale and wood tars over coal tar. Coal tar itself is a mixture of phenols, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and heterocyclic compounds, and the mechanism of the tars is unknown but may be antiproliferative.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 16; 11/03 T 893-912"};
WHY_AUDIO["S3-066"] = {tier:'B', text:"Methoxsalen acts by intercalating into D N A, inserting itself between the base pairs, and then crosslinking the strands when ultraviolet A light activates it; Doctor Sikazwe called it the only molecule he would mention with this chemical basis of action. Anthralin works instead through cell killing radicals, reactive molecules formed when it reacts with oxygen on its own, which is called autoxidation. Crisaborole binds the metal ions in the active site of phosphodiesterase four through its boron. Hydrocortisone, a glucocorticoid, binds its receptor by six hydrogen bonds.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 7, 17-18, 23; 11/03 T 963-967"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-004"] = {tier:'B', text:"Calcipotriene and calcitriol both enhance the absorption of calcium from the gut into the blood. Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D three, with two properties: an antiproliferative effect and calcium absorption. Calcipotriene was developed from it to keep the antiproliferative action and lose the calcium effect, but that did not succeed, so both are limited by hypercalcemia, too much calcium in the blood. No calcium effect for calcipotriene was the goal, not the result. Lowering blood calcium is wrong for both. Calcitriol having no calcium effect is wrong, because calcium absorption is one of its two properties.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 27; 11/04 T@2373-2758"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-057"] = {tier:'B', text:"Salicylic acid penetrates the skin a little deeper than glycolic acid because its aromatic ring makes it more lipophilic, meaning more fat soluble. Its log P, a measure of fat solubility, is about one to two, against about one for glycolic acid, which is hydrophilic and stays mostly on the skin surface. A much higher P K A is wrong; both have a P K A of about three, so their acid strength is similar. Being the smaller molecule is wrong; glycolic acid is the small molecule. Being a prodrug ester is wrong; salicylic acid is the acid itself.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 43, 64; 11/04 T@20564-21760"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-087"] = {tier:'B', text:"Flutamide is a non steroidal antiandrogen, a drug that opposes testosterone: it blocks testosterone from binding to its receptor, so testosterone cannot activate its pathways, and sebum production falls. Spironolactone is the steroidal antiandrogen, and it lowers testosterone biosynthesis instead. Being an azole antifungal and a strong C Y P three A four inhibitor describes ketoconazole. Being an estrogen that suppresses sebum in women describes the ethinyl estradiol in oral contraceptives.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 52-53; 11/04 T@32150-33140"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-102"] = {tier:'B', text:"Meradimate, an anthranilate, absorbs ultraviolet A like avobenzone, but it is degraded by light even faster. Avobenzone loses half its activity to light in about two hours, and Doctor Sikazwe said that with meradimate a user probably does not have even two hours. Being far more photostable is the reverse. Blocking ultraviolet B by reflection is wrong, because meradimate is an organic absorber; reflection is what the metal oxides zinc oxide and titanium dioxide do. High absorption through the skin was the concern raised for the benzophenones, not meradimate.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 61-62, 65; 11/04 T@40860, T@41158-41549"};
WHY_AUDIO["S4-109"] = {tier:'B', text:"From most to least photostable, the order is octocrylene, then ensulizole, then avobenzone. Photostability is measured by the photodegradation half life, the time light takes to break down half of the filter: about ninety hours for octocrylene, about twelve hours for ensulizole, and about two hours for avobenzone. The reversed order puts the least stable first. Putting ensulizole ahead of octocrylene is wrong, since ninety hours outlasts twelve. Putting avobenzone ahead of ensulizole is wrong, since two hours is shorter than twelve.",
  src:"teach; opt why; DPAW 62, 64-65; 11/04 T@40860, T@42930, T@44090"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-012"] = {tier:'B', text:"In Doctor Smith's lecture, ultraviolet B runs from two hundred ninety to three hundred twenty nanometers, and ultraviolet A from three hundred twenty to four hundred nanometers, so ultraviolet B is the shorter wavelength, more energetic band. Swapping the two ranges reverses them. Three hundred twenty to three hundred forty nanometers is the light range used in P U V A, which combines ultraviolet A light with light sensitizing drugs called psoralens; it is not ultraviolet B. Two hundred twenty to two hundred ninety shifts both bands too low; two hundred ninety to three hundred twenty is ultraviolet B.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 3, 8; sun audio 00:04:13-00:04:40; item note (Sikazwe's figures kept apart)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-013"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ultraviolet B is about ninety percent filtered by the ozone layer and does not pass through glass, while ultraviolet A is only minimally filtered by ozone and does pass through glass. Passing glass and being filtered by ozone the other way round reverses the two bands. Ultraviolet B penetrating further into the dermis is wrong; ultraviolet A goes deeper. Ultraviolet B being less energetic is wrong as well; ultraviolet A is the less energetic band, which is why it does not tend to cause sunburn.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8; sun audio 00:04:13-00:06:30"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-051"] = {tier:'B', text:"Substantivity is how well a sunscreen sticks to the skin and stays on through water and sweat, and the vehicle, the base the active ingredient is carried in, changes it. Alcohol gives the worst substantivity, so an alcohol based product does not stay on well, especially in water or with heavy sweating. Oil is the opposite: oils have the best substantivity, though they are not pleasant to use. Cream bases do better than alcohol bases. Polyacrylamide bases have good substantivity because they are not easily washed off.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 19; sun audio 00:18:35-00:18:55"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-057"] = {tier:'B', text:"Benzophenones are the sunscreen family with poor substantivity, meaning they do not stay on well through water and sweat, and they can cause sensitization, an allergic reaction of the skin; oxybenzone is an example. Anthranilates are the family used only in combination products to give broad ultraviolet coverage. Aminobenzoates are the most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B and may stain clothing. Dibenzoylmethanes are the family whose activity starts to decrease after fifteen minutes of use.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 24; sun audio 00:22:09-00:22:35"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-058"] = {tier:'B', text:"Aminobenzoates, the family of para aminobenzoic acid, P A B A, and padimate O, are the sunscreen family most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B rays, and they may stain clothing. Dibenzoylmethanes are the family whose activity starts to decrease after fifteen minutes of use. Cinnamates do not adhere to the skin well and depend on the vehicle to stay on. Benzophenones have poor substantivity and can cause sensitization, an allergic reaction of the skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 24; drug chart Sun Protection page"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-059"] = {tier:'B', text:"Anthranilates are the sunscreen family used only in combination products, together with other filters, to give broad ultraviolet coverage; menthyl anthranilate is an example. Aminobenzoates are wrong; they are the most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B. Salicylates are wrong; they do not adhere well and are easily removed by water or sweat. Benzophenones are wrong; they have poor substantivity and can cause sensitization, an allergic reaction of the skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 24; drug chart Sun Protection page"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-060"] = {tier:'B', text:"Dibenzoylmethanes, the family that includes avobenzone, are the sunscreens whose activity starts to decrease after fifteen minutes of use; no other family Doctor Smith compared carries a time point. Cinnamates are wrong; they do not adhere well and depend on the vehicle to stay on. Anthranilates are wrong; they are used only in combination products for broad ultraviolet coverage. Aminobenzoates are wrong; they are the most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 24; drug chart; item note (Sikazwe's half life is a different measure, not mixed)"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-061"] = {tier:'B', text:"Salicylates, such as homosalate, are the sunscreen family that does not adhere to the skin well and is easily removed by water or sweat. Cinnamates also adhere poorly, but they depend on the vehicle to stay on instead. Aminobenzoates are wrong; they are the most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B and may stain clothing. Dibenzoylmethanes are wrong; their activity starts to decrease after fifteen minutes. Anthranilates are wrong; they are used only in combination products.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 24; drug chart Sun Protection page"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-023"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ultraviolet A penetrates further into the dermis, the living layer beneath the outer skin, than ultraviolet B does. Ultraviolet A, from three hundred twenty to four hundred nanometers, is only minimally filtered by the ozone layer and passes through glass, while ultraviolet B is about ninety percent filtered by ozone and does not pass glass. Ultraviolet B going deeper is the reverse. Both reaching the same depth is wrong, since the depth differs. Neither passing the stratum corneum, the dead outer layer, is wrong, because ultraviolet A reaches the dermis and ultraviolet B burns living skin.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8, 11; sun audio 00:04:30-00:05:45"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-025"] = {tier:'B', text:"Ultraviolet A tends not to cause sunburn because it is a little less energetic than ultraviolet B; sunburn comes from ultraviolet B. That is also why the S P F number, which measures protection against burning, is a measure of ultraviolet B protection. Ozone filtering most of it out is wrong: ultraviolet A is only minimally filtered, and it is ultraviolet B that ozone filters about ninety percent of. Not passing glass is wrong; ultraviolet A passes glass. Stopping at the stratum corneum, the outer dead layer, is wrong; ultraviolet A penetrates further into the dermis than ultraviolet B.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 8, 13; sun audio 00:05:45-00:06:20, 00:11:30-00:11:50"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-043"] = {tier:'B', text:"An S P F thirty label tells the product's ultraviolet B protection, but not its ultraviolet A coverage, because S P F is built on the dose that causes sunburn, which comes from ultraviolet B. Blocking about ninety-seven percent of ultraviolet A is wrong; S P F thirty blocks about ninety-seven percent of ultraviolet B. A critical wavelength of at least three hundred seventy nanometers is what allows the broad spectrum label, which this product does not carry. A claim to lower skin cancer risk needs broad spectrum with S P F fifteen or greater.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 13-15; sun audio 00:10:30-00:14:00"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-066"] = {tier:'B', text:"Avobenzone and oxybenzone are organic sunscreens: they absorb ultraviolet radiation in the ultraviolet B or ultraviolet A range, or both, and convert it to heat energy. Avobenzone is a dibenzoylmethane and oxybenzone a benzophenone. Scattering and reflecting light back is the action of sunblocks, inorganic particles such as zinc oxide. Thickening the stratum corneum is wrong; the thickness of the outer skin affects tanning, but it is not how a sunscreen works. Speeding melanin production is an effect of psoralens, drugs activated by ultraviolet A light, not a sunscreen action.",
  src:"teach; opt why; Sun 3, 9, 20, 22, 24-25; drug chart; sun audio 00:21:05-00:21:30"};
WHY_AUDIO["L3C-067"] = {tier:'B', text:"Padimate O is an aminobenzoate, a P A B A derivative, the family most effective at absorbing ultraviolet B. Octyl methoxycinnamate is a cinnamate, which adheres poorly and depends on its vehicle. Homosalate is a salicylate, easily removed by water or sweat. Oxybenzone is a benzophenone, with poor substantivity and a risk of sensitization. Menthyl anthranilate is an anthranilate, used only in combination products. Avobenzone is a dibenzoylmethane, whose activity starts to fall after fifteen minutes. Zinc oxide is an oxide, a physical sunblock that scatters and reflects ultraviolet light, while the other six absorb it and release heat.",
  src:"pair whys; teach; item note (pairings from drug chart page one); Sun 20-24"};
